The process of making soap is saponification.
\text{Ester} + \text{Base} \longrightarrow \text{Alcohol} + \text{Soap}
In saponification, triglycerides are combined with a strong base to form fatty acid salts (soap) and glycerol (alcohol). This process involves the breaking of ester bonds by water in the presence of a base, which is characteristic of an alkaline hydrolysis reaction.

### Step 1: Calculate molar conductivity (\(\Lambda_{m}\))

The formula for molar conductivity is:
\Lambda_{m} = \frac{k}{C} \times 1000
Given specific conductance \(k = 5 \times 10^{-5}\) S \(cm^{-1}\) and concentration \(C = 0.0025\) M.
\Lambda_{m} = \frac{5 \times 10^{-5} \times 10^{3}}{0.0025} = \frac{5 \times 10^{-2}}{2.5 \times 10^{-3}}
\Lambda_{m} = 20 S cm^{2} mol^{-1}

### Step 2: Calculate degree of dissociation (\(\alpha\))

The degree of dissociation \(\alpha\) is given by the ratio of molar conductivity to limiting molar conductivity:
\alpha = \frac{\Lambda_{m}}{\Lambda_{m}^{0}}
Given limiting molar conductivity \(\Lambda_{m}^{0} = 400\) S \(cm^{2}\) \(mol^{-1}\).
\alpha = \frac{20}{400} = \frac{1}{20}

### Step 3: Calculate dissociation constant (\(K_{a}\))

For a weak acid, the dissociation constant \(K_{a}\) is given by Ostwald's dilution law:
K_{a} = \frac{C\alpha^{2}}{1-\alpha}
Substitute the values of \(C\) and \(\alpha\):
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025 \times (\frac{1}{20})^{2}}{1 - \frac{1}{20}}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025 \times \frac{1}{400}}{\frac{19}{20}}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025}{400} \times \frac{20}{19}
K_{a} = \frac{0.0025}{20 \times 19} = \frac{0.0025}{380}
K_{a} \approx 6.5789 \times 10^{-6}
To express this in the format \(\times 10^{-7}\):
K_{a} = 65.789 \times 10^{-7}
Rounding to the nearest integer, the dissociation constant is \(66 \times 10^{-7}\).

### 1) Ozonolysis of alkene

Ozonolysis of an alkene followed by reductive workup (Zn, H₂O) results in the cleavage of the C=C double bond to form aldehydes and/or ketones. It does not produce alcohols.
\text{>C=C<} \xrightarrow[\text{Zn, H}_2\text{O}]{\text{O}_3} \text{-C(=O)- , -C(=O)-}

### 2) Hydroboration – oxidation of alkene

This reaction converts an alkene into an alcohol. It is a correct method for alcohol preparation.
\text{R}_1\text{R}_2\text{C=CH}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{BH}_3} \text{R}_1-\underset{\text{R}_2}{\underset{|}{\text{C}}}H-\text{CH}_2-\text{BH}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{OH}^-} \text{R}_1-\underset{\text{R}_2}{\underset{|}{\text{C}}}H-\text{CH}_2-\text{OH}

### 3) Reaction of alkyl halide with aqueous NaOH

This is a nucleophilic substitution reaction where an alkyl halide reacts with aqueous NaOH to form an alcohol. It is a correct method.
\text{R-X} + \text{NaOH} \longrightarrow \text{R-OH} + \text{NaX}

### 4) Reaction of Ketone with RMgBr followed by hydrolysis

A ketone reacts with a Grignard reagent (RMgBr) followed by hydrolysis to produce a tertiary alcohol. This is a correct method.
\text{R}_1-\overset{\text{O}}{\overset{||}{\text{C}}}-\text{R}_2 + \text{R'MgX} \longrightarrow \text{R}_1-\underset{\text{OMgX}}{\underset{|}{\overset{\text{R'}}{\overset{|}{\text{C}}}}}-\text{R}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{H}_3\text{O}^+} \text{R}_1-\underset{\text{OH}}{\underset{|}{\overset{\text{R'}}{\overset{|}{\text{C}}}}}-\text{R}_2 + \text{Mg(OH)X}
Since ozonolysis of an alkene yields carbonyl compounds and not alcohols, it is the incorrect method of preparation among the given options.

